<!------------------------------------------------ COOKIE_SESSION_ARCHITECTURE ------------------------------------------------>

<div class="container my-5">

  <div class="card shadow-lg border-0 rounded-4">

    <!-- Header -->

    <div class="card-header bg-secondary text-white p-4">

      <h2 class="fw-bold mb-1">

        🍪 Cookie & Session Architecture

      </h2>

      <p class="mb-0">

        Understand how Browser Cookies and Express Sessions communicate.

      </p>

    </div>

    <div class="card-body">

      <div class="alert alert-warning">

        Express Session stores user data on the <strong>Server</strong>.
        The browser stores only the <strong>Session ID (connect.sid)</strong>
        inside a Cookie.

      </div>

      <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-4 rounded">

            USER LOGIN

                 │
                 ▼

        Browser Sends Login Request

                 │
                 ▼

          Express Server

                 │
                 ▼

      Validate Email & Password

                 │
                 ▼

       Create Express Session

                 │
                 ▼

Server Stores Session Data

req.session = {

    user : {...},

    isLogin : true,

    loginTime : ...

}

                 │
                 ▼

Generate Session ID

Example:

abc123xyz789

                 │
                 ▼

Browser Receives Cookie

connect.sid = abc123xyz789

                 │
                 ▼

Every New Request

Browser Sends

connect.sid

                 │
                 ▼

Server Finds Session

                 │
                 ▼

User Authenticated

</pre>

      <hr>

      <h3 class="mb-4">

        🔄 Browser ↔ Server Communication

      </h3>

      <pre class="bg-primary text-white p-4 rounded">

Browser

    │

    │ GET /dashboard

    │ Cookie

    │ connect.sid

    ▼

Express Server

    │

    │ Find Session

    ▼

Session Store

    │

    ▼

req.session.user

    │

    ▼

Dashboard Response

</pre>

      <hr>

      <h3>

        📊 Cookie vs Session

      </h3>

      <div class="table-responsive mt-3">

        <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

          <thead class="table-dark">

            <tr>

              <th>

                Cookie

              </th>

              <th>

                Session

              </th>

            </tr>

          </thead>

          <tbody>

            <tr>

              <td>

                Stored in Browser

              </td>

              <td>

                Stored on Server

              </td>

            </tr>

            <tr>

              <td>

                Small Data

              </td>

              <td>

                Large Data

              </td>

            </tr>

            <tr>

              <td>

                Can be modified by user

              </td>

              <td>

                Controlled by Server

              </td>

            </tr>

            <tr>

              <td>

                Stores connect.sid

              </td>

              <td>

                Stores User Information

              </td>

            </tr>

            <tr>

              <td>

                Sent with every request

              </td>

              <td>

                Retrieved using Session ID

              </td>

            </tr>

            <tr>

              <td>

                Less Secure

              </td>

              <td>

                More Secure

              </td>

            </tr>

          </tbody>

        </table>

      </div>

      <hr>

      <div class="alert alert-success">

        <h5>

          💡 Key Points

        </h5>

        <ul class="mb-0">

          <li>Browser stores only <strong>connect.sid</strong>.</li>

          <li>Server stores the complete <strong>req.session</strong> object.</li>

          <li>Every request automatically sends the Cookie.</li>

          <li>The Session ID is used to retrieve the correct session.</li>

          <li>If the Session expires, the user must log in again.</li>

        </ul>

      </div>

    </div>

  </div>

</div>


